---
import type { Project } from '../data/portfolio';
import { dataGPTMedia as media, dataGPTPersonas, researchOutcomes, competitors, competitorOutcomes, models, features, queryResults, contextResults, conclusions } from '../data/datagpt-case-study';
import ProjectIdentity from './ProjectIdentity.astro';
import SkillTag from './SkillTag.astro';
import Stat from './Stat.astro';
import CaseStudyMedia from './CaseStudyMedia.astro';
import CaseStudyComparison from './CaseStudyComparison.astro';
import '../styles/datagpt-case-study.css';
interface Props { project: Project; }
const { project } = Astro.props;
const researchMetrics = [
  { value: '30+', label: 'stakeholder interviews', note: '' },
  { value: '4', label: 'user types synthesised', note: '' },
  { value: '4', label: 'competitor groups mapped', note: '' },
];
---
<article class="case-study-content case-study-content--datagpt" data-case-content>
  <div class="case-study-artwork">
    <video data-case-video muted loop playsinline preload="none" poster={media.hero.src} width={media.heroVideo.width} height={media.heroVideo.height} style={`aspect-ratio: ${media.heroVideo.width} / ${media.heroVideo.height}`} aria-label={media.hero.alt}>
      <source data-src={media.heroVideo.src} type="video/mp4" />
      <img src={media.hero.src} width={media.heroVideo.width} height={media.heroVideo.height} alt={media.hero.alt} decoding="async" />
    </video>
  </div>
  <div class="case-study-body dg-case">
    <section class="dg-intro" aria-labelledby="dg-title">
      <div class="dg-identity">
        <ProjectIdentity title={project.title} logo={project.logo} />
        <h1 id="dg-title" class="sr-only" data-case-title>DataGPT</h1>
        <div class="project-tags">{project.tags.map(label => <SkillTag label={label} variant="raised" />)}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="dg-summary"><p>{project.outcome}</p><p>{project.description}</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-role" aria-label="Project role and team">
      <div><h2 class="dg-label">My role</h2><p>Solo product designer</p><p>I owned the product end to end:</p><ul><li>Discovery and stakeholder interviews</li><li>Personas and competitor mapping</li><li>Prototypes and usability testing</li><li>UI, design system, developer hand-off</li><li>Pilot and post-release testing</li><li>Website, promo videos and marketing materials</li></ul></div>
      <div><h2 class="dg-label">Team</h2><ul><li>Product owner</li><li>Product manager</li><li>Engineering team</li></ul></div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-problem">
      <p class="dg-label">Problem</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-problem">Dashboards showed what happened.<br />Nobody could simply ask why.</h2>
      <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-block">
        <div class="dg-copy-stack"><div><h3>Business context</h3><p>Companies spend heavily on BI tools, but getting from data to a decision still went through a data team. Dashboards were static, and business users couldn't ask follow-up questions.</p></div><div><h3>User pains</h3><p>Executives waited for the next meeting to learn why revenue moved. Analysts were "drowning in ad hoc requests". Marketers pieced a story together from dozens of reports.</p></div><div class="dg-goal"><h3>Goal</h3><p>Let anyone ask a complex question in plain English and get an answer they can trust.</p></div></div>
        <div class="dg-card dg-voices">
          <div class="dg-voice"><span class="dg-avatar" aria-hidden="true">E</span><div><h3>Executive</h3><blockquote>Why did revenue drop? I don't want to <strong>wait for the next meeting for answers.</strong> Revenue opportunities are going missed.</blockquote></div></div>
          <div class="dg-voice"><span class="dg-avatar" aria-hidden="true">DT</span><div><h3>Data team</h3><blockquote>We <strong>spend so much time on dashboard building</strong> and maintenance, and next week the questions change.</blockquote></div></div>
          <div class="dg-voice"><span class="dg-avatar" aria-hidden="true">A</span><div><h3>Analyst</h3><blockquote>We are <strong>drowning in ad hoc requests.</strong></blockquote></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-research">
      <p class="dg-label">Discovery &amp; Research</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-research">Traditional BI was too slow and too complex for everyday decisions</h2>
      <p class="dg-lede">I started with stakeholder interviews and a review of the conversational-analytics market. Executives, data analysts, marketers and product managers described the same problem. They didn't want more charts. They wanted answers on demand.</p>
      <div class="dg-block"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.research} /></div>
      <dl class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-research-stats">{researchMetrics.map(metric => <Stat {...metric} />)}</dl>
      <h3 class="dg-outcome-label">Outcome</h3><div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three">{researchOutcomes.map(([title, body]) => <article class="dg-card"><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div>
      <div class="dg-subsection"><h3 class="dg-copy-width">Two groups: the people who ask questions and the people who make answers possible</h3><p class="dg-copy-width">Most users only ask questions. A smaller group connects the data and defines what the metrics mean. DataGPT had to serve both.</p>
        <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-personas">{dataGPTPersonas.map((persona, i) => <article class:list={['dg-card dg-persona', i === 3 && 'dg-persona--wide']}>
          <div class="dg-card-heading"><h3>{persona.title}</h3><span class="dg-badge">{persona.group}</span></div><p>{persona.description}</p><blockquote class="dg-question">{persona.question}</blockquote>
          <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-needs"><div><h4>Need</h4><p>{persona.need}</p></div><div><h4>Frustration</h4><p>{persona.frustration}</p></div></div>
        </article>)}</div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-competitors">
      <p class="dg-label">Competitor mapping</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-competitors">Many "AI analytics" tools, few that explain why</h2><p class="dg-lede">We mapped the landscape of “conversational analytics” tools – from established BI giants to emerging AI solutions:</p>
      <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-block">{competitors.map(([title, body, logo]) => <article class="dg-card"><div class="dg-competitor-heading"><img src={logo} alt="" width="40" height="40" /><h3>{title}</h3></div><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div>
      <div class="dg-block"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.competitors} /></div>
      <h3 class="dg-outcome-label">Outcome</h3><div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three">{competitorOutcomes.map(([title, body]) => <article class="dg-card"><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-models">
      <p class="dg-label">Chat or dashboard?</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-models">I prototyped two interaction models and we shipped a hybrid</h2><p class="dg-lede">Before committing to a direction, I tested two ways for people to work with their data through AI.</p>
      <div class="dg-block"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.prototypes} /></div>
      <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-block">{models.map((model, i) => <article class:list={['dg-card dg-model', i === 2 && 'dg-model--shipped']} data-theme={i === 2 ? 'dark' : undefined}><div class="dg-card-heading"><h3>{model.title}</h3><span class="dg-badge">{model.label}</span></div><p>{model.body}</p><p class="dg-accent">{model.quote}</p>{model.outcome && <p class="dg-muted">{model.outcome}</p>}</article>)}</div>
    </section>
    <section class="dg-dark" aria-labelledby="dg-shipped" data-theme="dark">
      <div class="dg-dark-inner"><p class="dg-label">What shipped?</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-shipped">AI analyst to talk to</h2>
        {features.map(feature => <div class="dg-feature"><div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two"><div><h3>{feature.title}</h3><p>{feature.body}</p></div><ul class="dg-accent">{feature.points.map(point => <li>{point}</li>)}</ul></div><CaseStudyMedia {...feature.media} /></div>)}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-pilot">
      <p class="dg-label">Pilot launch</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-pilot">After launch we surfaced <span class="dg-accent">two problems.</span></h2><p class="dg-lede">We rolled DataGPT out to a small group of pilot customers, including Papa John's, to test usability, accuracy and fit with their workflows before full release. I ran testing during the pilot and again after release.</p>
      <div class="dg-block"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.pilot} /></div>
      <div class="dg-subsection"><p class="dg-label dg-accent">1 Problem</p><h3 class="dg-display">Ambiguous questions got confident but wrong answers</h3>
        <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-block"><article class="dg-card"><h4>Signal</h4><p>Pilot users often phrased questions in ways that could be read more than one way.</p><p class="dg-question">What's my best-selling product?</p><p class="dg-answer">Coffee is your best-selling product.</p></article><article class="dg-card"><h4>Cause</h4><p>"Best-selling" could mean most units, most revenue or highest margin. The model picked units, while the user meant revenue. The answer was technically correct but wrong for the question, so people had to rephrase or ask follow-ups, and insights took longer.</p></article></div>
        <div class="dg-subsection"><h4>The fix was LLM instructions (skills)</h4><p class="dg-copy-width">I designed a way for admins to add business logic and definitions in the Schema Editor. For example, <strong class="dg-accent">"conversion"</strong> means the conversion-rate metric, and <strong class="dg-accent">"best-performing product"</strong> means highest revenue.</p><div class="dg-media-gap"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.instructions} /></div></div>
        <h4 class="dg-outcome-label">Results</h4><dl class="dg-grid dg-grid--four dg-results">{queryResults.map(metric => <Stat {...metric} />)}</dl>
      </div>
      <div class="dg-subsection"><p class="dg-label dg-accent">2 Problem</p><h3 class="dg-display">Users couldn't see which dates and filters an answer used</h3>
        <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-block"><article class="dg-card"><h4>Cause</h4><p>The date picker and filters lived only in the Data Navigator. When it was collapsed, they disappeared. <strong class="dg-accent">"Show me revenue"</strong> might use <strong class="dg-accent">"last 30 days"</strong>, and nothing in the chat said so. The numbers were right, but the missing context made people doubt them.</p></article><article class="dg-card"><h4>Fix</h4><p>A persistent global header above both Chat and Data Navigator:</p><ul><li>Moved the date picker and filter chips into a top bar.</li><li>Kept them in sync between Chat and Navigator.</li><li>Added subtle motion when dates or filters change.</li></ul></article></div>
        <div class="dg-media-gap"><CaseStudyComparison before={media.before} after={media.after} label="Compare date and filter visibility" /></div>
        <h4 class="dg-outcome-label">Results</h4><dl class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-results">{contextResults.map(metric => <Stat {...metric} />)}</dl>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="dg-dark" data-theme="dark" aria-labelledby="dg-impact">
      <div class="dg-dark-inner"><div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two"><p class="dg-label">Impact first year</p><div><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-impact">$0 <span class="dg-accent">→</span> $1.5M ARR</h2><p>DataGPT's first year: 20% month-over-month revenue growth and zero customer churn in the last six months.</p></div></div>
        <div class="dg-grid dg-grid--two dg-block dg-testimonials">
          <figure class="dg-card"><figcaption><strong>Dan Calzone</strong><br />Director of Growth, Plex</figcaption><blockquote>Other BI tools show you what happened. DataGPT helps you understand why and what to do about it. It's like having a personal data analyst at my fingertips.</blockquote><p>Customers reported a 60% average reduction in pending data requests. Teams that used to wait days for an answer got insights daily, even hourly. Adoption spread on its own: departments outside the original use case started using DataGPT, because it needed no training. You just ask a question.</p></figure>
          <figure class="dg-card"><figcaption><strong>Mia Chen</strong><br />International Analyst, Papa John's</figcaption><blockquote>With DataGPT, we finally have a more complete view of our retail data across web and app, giving us a clear view of customer journeys, purchase behaviours, and product performance, all in one place.</blockquote><p>In the UK division, DataGPT became a standard part of weekly executive meetings. Automating daily and weekly performance summaries freed analysts from more than 20 hours of manual reporting a week and got insights to marketing and operations faster.</p></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-website"><p class="dg-label">Extending the Experience</p><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-website">Website</h2><p class="dg-lede">As DataGPT grew from a prototype into a full platform, it needed a public face that explained its value. I designed the website, the promo videos and the marketing materials. The site uses the same clarity and explainability as the product.</p><div class="dg-block"><CaseStudyMedia {...media.website} /></div></section>
    <section aria-labelledby="dg-conclusion"><h2 class="dg-display" id="dg-conclusion">Conclusion</h2><p class="dg-lede">I focused on designing an intuitive, conversational analytics experience that makes data exploration accessible to both technical and non technical users. By simplifying how people ask, understand, and validate data, i helped DataGPT evolve from a chat concept into a transparent, insight driven workspace.</p><p class="dg-copy-width dg-conclusion-lead"><strong>Through iterative testing and real world feedback, we introduced key features that shaped the product’s maturity:</strong></p><div class="dg-grid dg-grid--three dg-media-gap">{conclusions.map(([title, body]) => <article class="dg-card"><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div></section>
  </div>
</article>

<script>
  import { initializeCaseStudyVideos } from '../scripts/case-study-videos';
  initializeCaseStudyVideos();
</script>
